# Box-local systemd configuration, DECLARED.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Part 25's rule is that the VPS runs code and never edits it, so a repo unit is
# canonical and an installed copy that differs from it is drift. The drift check
# in scripts/check_heartbeat_cron.sh enforces that, and it also reports a
# `<unit>.d/*.conf` drop-in as drift -- because a drop-in wins over the unit file
# and is invisible to any byte comparison of the unit alone, which is exactly how
# a box-local workaround outlives the defect it worked around.
#
# But some configuration is GENUINELY box-local and always will be. Where this
# machine keeps its state directory is a property of this machine, not of the
# system, and there is nothing for the repo to say about it. Reporting that
# forever as drift has a specific cost: the heartbeat sits permanently at exit 8
# with `unit_drift`, and an alarm that is always on is an alarm nobody reads. The
# genuine case -- somebody hand-edited an installed unit and a pull will never
# fix it -- then arrives into a channel already trained to be ignored.
#
# So: a drop-in is forgiven when this file says which unit may have one AND which
# directives it may set. Anything else in it is still drift. The difference
# between a declared override and an undeclared one is the whole point; a switch
# that turned the check off would be worse than the noise.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT DOES *NOT* BELONG HERE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Anything the repo could have said itself. If a directive would be correct on
# every box, it goes in the unit, not here -- this file is the exception list and
# a long exception list is a repo that has stopped describing its own deployment.
#
# Two things were removed from the box's drop-ins on exactly that ground:
#
#   EnvironmentFile=-%h/chester-reports/.env
#       Already in chester-eod.service and chester-heartbeat.service. The
#       drop-ins were restating a line the repo units carry, so the drop-in copies
#       are redundant and are deleted rather than declared.
#
#   ReadWritePaths=%h/state
#       Now `-%h/state` in the repo units. The leading '-' makes it
#       permitted-if-present, so a box that does not use ~/state is unaffected
#       while this one stops needing a drop-in to be able to write there.
#
# That leaves Environment= as the only genuinely box-local directive, which is
# the right size for this list.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FORMAT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   <unit-name>  <permitted directive key> [<key> ...]
#
# Keys are matched on the part LEFT OF THE FIRST '=' in each drop-in line.
# Comments, blank lines and [Section] headers are ignored in a drop-in. A drop-in
# line whose key is not listed for its unit is drift, and so is a drop-in on a
# unit absent from this file.

# The EOD pass and the monitor that watches it. This box keeps heartbeat, status
# and lock files in ~/state rather than the wrappers' ~/.chester default, and the
# monitor MUST agree with the pipeline about where they are -- a checker pointed
# at the wrong directory reports CRITICAL/no_heartbeat forever while everything
# runs perfectly, which is the false alarm this whole mechanism exists to avoid.
chester-eod.service           Environment
chester-heartbeat.service     Environment

# The 07:00 morning chain, for the same reason and with a sharper edge: the
# morning heartbeat is READ by chester-heartbeat.service (exit 5 when a 07:00 was
# owed and none arrived). If the writer used ~/.chester while the reader used
# ~/state, the check would fire every single morning and the first thing it ever
# reported would be a lie about a report that had in fact been delivered.
chester-overnight.service     Environment
chester-morning-anchor.service Environment
